# models/file

The metadata index of a blog's folder: a record for every file and every directory in it, so the S3 backend of `storage/folder` can stat, list, walk and resolve case without asking S3. It is a sibling of `models/entry`, and a file and its entry join on the same path string. See [#2258](https://github.com/blotcms/blot/issues/2258) ("Metadata index (Redis): `models/file`").

- `storage/folder`'s S3 backend (`app/storage/folder/s3.js`) is its only writer. Nothing else calls the write functions. It is the only backend of `storage/folder`, so every blog's folder has an index.
- It is derived from S3: the backend writes to S3 first and to the index after, so a crash leaves the index behind S3, never ahead. It can be rebuilt from a full listing at any time (`rebuild`, and `scripts/storage/rebuild-file-index.js`).
- Writes read what they need, then apply everything in one `multi()`/`exec()`. That is only safe because each blog has one writer at a time, the sync lock (`app/sync/lock.js`). No Lua and no `WATCH`.

---

## Records

`{ type: "file" | "dir", size, modified, sha1, flags }`: size in bytes, modified in milliseconds since the epoch, sha1 the SHA-1 of the file's bytes in hex. A directory has size 0 and no hash. `sha1` is `""` when it isn't known, which only a rebuild without hashing produces. `flags` is there only for a file which has some (see Flags below).

Paths are normalised with `helper/pathNormalizer`, exactly as entry keys are (`models/entry/key.js`): a leading slash, no trailing slash, no double slashes, NFC. The folder itself is `/`, always exists, and has no record of its own (`get` returns a directory with `modified` 0).

---

## Redis keys

No key per file. There is a hash, a lookup and a sorted set per order (see Natural order) per directory, and two keys per blog. Every key starts `blog:{blogID}:file`, so blog deletion's `blog:{blogID}:*` sweep takes it with the rest.

| Key | Type | Contents |
|-----|------|----------|
| `blog:{blogID}:file:dir:{dirPath}` | hash | child name → packed record `type\|size\|modified\|sha1` |
| `blog:{blogID}:file:lower:{dirPath}` | hash | lowercased child name → the child names that fold to it, joined by `/` and sorted |
| `blog:{blogID}:file:sort:v1:{order}:{dirPath}` | sorted set | the directory's children in one of the orders in `sorts.js`, all at score 0 (see Natural order below). Today the one order is `all`: every child, in natural name order |
| `blog:{blogID}:file:paths` | sorted set | every path below the root, all at score 0, for `ZRANGEBYLEX` subtree queries |
| `blog:{blogID}:file:totals` | hash | `bytes`, `files`: the folder's totals, for the storage quota |

For a folder holding `/Posts/a.txt` (5 bytes), `/posts/b.txt` (3 bytes) and an empty directory `/drafts`:

```
blog:X:file:dir:/          Posts  -> d|0|1700000000000|
                           posts  -> d|0|1700000000000|
                           drafts -> d|0|1700000000000|
blog:X:file:lower:/        posts  -> Posts/posts
                           drafts -> drafts
blog:X:file:sort:v1:all:/  drafts Posts posts   (each as its member)
blog:X:file:dir:/Posts     a.txt  -> f|5|1700000000000|aaf4c61d...
blog:X:file:lower:/Posts   a.txt  -> a.txt
blog:X:file:sort:v1:all:/Posts  a.txt
blog:X:file:dir:/posts     b.txt  -> f|3|1700000000000|e9d71f5e...
blog:X:file:lower:/posts   b.txt  -> b.txt
blog:X:file:sort:v1:all:/posts  b.txt
blog:X:file:paths          /Posts /Posts/a.txt /drafts /posts /posts/b.txt
blog:X:file:totals         bytes -> 8, files -> 2
```

The packing is the record type's first letter (`f` or `d`), then size, modified and sha1, separated by `|`, then, for a flagged file only, `|` and its flags as JSON (`f|0|1700000000000|da39a3ee...|[{"code":"SYNC_TOO_LARGE","size":200000000,"limit":100000000}]`). Everything after the fourth `|` is the JSON, which may hold a `|` of its own. Flags which fail to parse read as none. A name may contain `|`, but it is only ever a field, never part of a value. No name can contain `/`, so it separates the names in the lowercased lookup. An empty directory has no hash of its own; its record in its parent's hash is what makes it exist.

Case-insensitive resolution looks each segment up in its directory's lowercased lookup. Names which differ only by case (`/Posts` and `/posts`) share one field, so neither hides the other: the exact name wins, otherwise the first in sorted order, by `helper/caseSensitivePath.pick` over the stored names. Each segment is resolved on its own, without going back to try a sibling, as a file system does.

Subtree queries use the bounds `[/dir/` and `(/dir0` (`0` is the character after `/`), so `/dir b` and `/dir0` stay out.

### Paging a directory

Listings are in natural name order, the order of each directory's `all` sorted set (see Natural order below). `list` reads a page with `ZRANGEBYLEX` on that set, from just after the member of the name `after` (which `key.sortMember` gives from the name alone, so a cursor need not name an entry still there), one more than the limit to tell whether another page follows, then `HMGET`s the page's records from the hash: a page costs the same however large the directory. `range` reads a page by position instead, for pages a person picks by number: the visible children (not hidden everywhere) are the first ranks of the set, so their total is one `ZLEXCOUNT` and the page one `ZRANGE` by rank.

A directory without the set (none has one until a writer or the backfill builds it) is read whole from its hash and sorted the same way, comparing members as bytes with `key.compareSorted`, so it lists in exactly the set's order.

Paging through the path set instead needs no new key, but a directory's children are interleaved there with all their descendants, so skipping each subdirectory's subtree costs a round trip per subdirectory on the page.

Before the sorted sets, listings read a names set per directory (`blog:{blogID}:file:names:{dirPath}`, the children in code-unit order). Nothing writes one now. The writers still delete a directory's names set with the directory, since code from before could make one during a deploy, until `scripts/storage/remove-file-index-names.js` has removed the rest.

### Natural order

Each directory has a sorted set per order in `sorts.js`, written by the same writers in the same `exec`. Listings read the `all` set.

A member is a group, a sort key, NUL and the name (`key.sortMember`), so byte order of members is the order a file browser shows:

- The group is `\x01` for a name shown somewhere and `\x02` for one hidden everywhere (a dotfile, a `.preview.html`), so the visible children are one range of ranks.
- The sort key is the name in NFD, without the combining diacritics U+0300 to U+036F and without control characters, lowercased, with each run of digits written as `\x01`, its length without leading zeros (as the code point length + 1), the digits without leading zeros, and `0x1000` minus the number of leading zeros (one code point). So `IMG_2` sorts before `IMG_10`, `café` beside `cafe`, digits before letters and punctuation, and more leading zeros first, as the dashboard's `helper/alphanum` orders names. Leading spaces stay, so the names that start with `_` are one run.
- The name breaks ties, so names which differ only in case keep code-unit order, uppercase first, and `key.nameOfSorted` reads it back. A path holding NUL is refused.

`ZRANGEBYLEX` compares bytes, so code compares members with `key.compareSorted` (`Buffer.compare`), never `<`. The `v1` in the key is the member format: changing it means a new key and a backfill.

A writer keeps a set complete: a directory with a hash but no set gets one built whole on its first new child. Code from before the sets, running beside the new code during a deploy or after a rollback, can still change a directory without them, so a set which exists is not trusted until checked: `checkSorts(blogID, { apply })` compares every directory's set with its hash member by member, and with `apply` rebuilds each one which is missing or differs, under another key (`sort:build`) renamed into place, so a run stopped part way never leaves a partial set. `scripts/storage/add-file-index-sorts.js` runs it for every blog under its sync lock, and estimates the sets' memory.

---

## Flags

A flag records something that went wrong with a file which can't be worked out again from its path or the blog's settings: a placeholder written because the file couldn't be fetched, a failed build, a broken image reference. `flags.js` is the dictionary of codes. Each code has a source (the writer which sets it), a message for the person, the detail keys it may carry, and whether it marks a placeholder or is transient. Anything that follows from the path or settings (public, dot and underscore files, file types Blot doesn't build) is not stored.

- A flag is `{ code, ...detail }`. Detail is capped at 20 targets, with `count` holding the full number, and 200 characters per string, so one file can't swell its directory's hash.
- Writing a file replaces its record, so its flags go with its old bytes. A move keeps them.
- Each source replaces all of its own flags each time it runs (`setFlags`) and leaves other sources' flags alone. With `{ sha1 }` it only flags bytes it saw.
- Readers ignore codes they don't know, so units on either side of a deploy can share records. Writers refuse them.
- A placeholder is an empty file with a flag whose code is marked `placeholder`. That is what tells it from a file which really is empty.
- A rebuild keeps the flags of files whose size (and SHA-1, where both are known) is unchanged. If the index itself is lost, a resync brings sync flags back and the next build brings back build flags.

---

## Functions

All return promises.

| Function | |
|----------|---|
| `get(blogID, path)` | The record, or null |
| `list(blogID, dir, { after, limit })` | One page of a directory in natural name order, starting after the name `after`: `{ entries: [{ name, ...record }], next }`, next the `after` of the following page, or null after the last. `limit` defaults to 1000. Null if dir is not a directory |
| `listAll(blogID, dir)` | Every entry of a directory, in listing order, or null. Pages through `list`, but holds the whole directory |
| `range(blogID, dir, { offset, limit, order })` | A page of the visible children (not dotfiles or `.preview.html`) by position: `{ entries, total }`, `order` `asc` or `desc`. Null if dir is not a directory |
| `subtree(blogID, dir, { pageSize })` | Async iterator over every path below dir, in byte order of the whole path |
| `resolveCaseInsensitive(blogID, path)` | The stored spelling of path, or null |
| `totals(blogID)` | `{ bytes, files }` |
| `set(blogID, path, record)`, `setMany(blogID, [{ path, ...record }])` | Record paths, giving every ancestor without a record a directory record (S3 has implicit directories). Refuses a path below a file (ENOTDIR), a file over a directory (EISDIR) and a directory over a file (EEXIST) |
| `setFlags(blogID, path, source, flags, { sha1 })` | Replace one source's flags on a file, keeping the rest of its record. With sha1, only if the record still has that hash. Resolves to false if there is no such file, or the hash differs |
| `flagged(blogID, dir)` | Async iterator of `{ path, ...record }` for every flagged file below dir. Reads each directory's hash once, so it is for rebuilds and scripts |
| `remove(blogID, path)` | Forget a file, or a directory and its whole subtree. Resolves to the removed record, or null |
| `removeAll(blogID)` | Forget the whole folder |
| `checkSorts(blogID, { apply, order })` | Async iterator of `{ dir, order, state, expected, card, bytes, longest }` per directory and order: `state` is `ok`, `missing` or `mismatched`. With `apply`, rebuilds each set which isn't ok; must run under the sync lock then |
| `rebuild(blogID, records)` | Replace the index with records from a full listing (an iterable or async iterable). Resolves to `{ files, dirs, bytes, skipped }`. A record which conflicts with one before it (below a file, or a file and a directory at one path, which S3 can hold and a file system cannot) is skipped and listed in `skipped` as `{ path, type, code }`. Flags of unchanged files are carried over |

Removing a directory goes through its subtree deepest first, a page at a time, so each file's size is still in its parent's hash when the totals are corrected. If it stops part way, what is left is consistent apart from a lowercased lookup which may still name a child already gone, until its directory goes too.

---

## Files

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `index.js` | The functions above |
| `key.js` | Key names, the path normalisation, and how a name is spelled in a sorted set |
| `sorts.js` | The orders a directory's children are indexed in |
| `record.js` | Packs and parses records |
| `flags.js` | The dictionary of flag codes, and how flags are capped and replaced |
| `tests/` | Specs, against real Redis |
